#!/bin/sh
# Pre-push gate: block a push if the framework health checks fail (schema drift, broken primitives).
# Bypass with `git push --no-verify` when you know better.
#
# Installed via `make install-hooks` (sets core.hooksPath to .githooks).

# craft-coverage is here because a doc-only commit never runs an authoring gate. The doc index went red
# for weeks with four orphaned guides and a link to a file nobody wrote, and nothing noticed, because the
# only thing that checked it was a command you had to remember to type. This is the place it cannot be
# skipped by forgetting. It is pure file reads, so it costs about a second.
# doc-refs joined it once engine-doctrine/MISTAKES.md was patched. Same argument: a doc names a command as an
# instruction, and an instruction that fails teaches the author to distrust the whole file. It found
# 111 usage strings printing a path that had moved months earlier.
# skill-reach is the same argument once more, one layer up: a SKILL.md nothing routes to is unreachable
# the same way a broken doc link is, and it is invisible from a diff, because the new skill file itself
# looks finished. vawe-review-loop shipped that way and carried the judge loop's stopping rule with it.
# arsenal-check is the same argument once more, and it belongs here rather than in author-check for a
# specific reason: the commit that ADDS a capability is a framework commit, and framework commits never
# run an authoring ladder. The three.js layer landed that way and stayed out of the catalogue for as
# long as it existed, so the author who needed a globe went to npm instead (engine-doctrine/MISTAKES.md #335).
# scenes-json is here rather than in a site build because the drift it catches is INVISIBLE: the site
# serves its own copy of a scene, so a film fixed in films/scene/ keeps playing broken on the site
# until someone notices by hand. Nobody did for forty days (engine-doctrine/MISTAKES.md #475). It reads 15 files.
# mistakes-check is here for the same reason as doc-refs: MISTAKES.md is edited on almost every commit
# that touches this file's own doctrine, and a duplicate entry is only visible across the whole log, not
# from the diff being pushed. It reads one file.
# generated-check is here because THREE derived artefacts had no drift check and the repo had already
# written this idea twice for two others (craft-coverage for the doc index, scenes-json for the site's
# scene copies), each after its own incident. site/lib/arsenal.json had nobody, went 67 items stale and
# kept listing a sound cue that had been deleted from the engine. Running every generator and diffing is
# the general form of both, so a fourth artefact cannot arrive with nobody watching it.
# discovery is here because a capability nobody can find is a capability nobody uses, and it broke three
# ways in one week without any run going red: 131 registry entries with no blurb, 95 of 95 block
# families missing from the search corpus entirely, and 33 entries arriving from the catalogue where
# the load-time refusal cannot reach them. It also compares the CLI index against the website's, which
# disagreed by 185 entries while both looked healthy. Pure reads, about a second.
# prop-probe is here because the thing it checks is a property of the ENGINE, not of any one film, and
# nothing in an authoring ladder ever exercises a prop no film uses. That is the whole gap it closes:
# `metalness` and `roughness` were declared on the three layer and overwritten by literals, and every
# gate stayed green for months because no scene set them (engine-doctrine/MISTAKES.md #565). It is the slow step
# here, about 35 seconds, and it is the only one that needs a browser.
# seo-surface is here for the same reason generated-check is: sitemap.ts, robots.ts, seo.ts,
# ogCard.tsx and schema.ts each generate a fact (a canonical, a share card, a sitemap URL, a JSON-LD
# object) that nothing else checks at write time, and every one of them is invisible in a diff. Pure
# file reads plus running schema.ts's own builders; no browser, no build.
# coverage joins the list for the same reason doc-refs does, and needs the same full local library to
# mean anything: quality/gates/coverage.mjs asks whether any AUTHORED scene actually USES a piece of
# engine vocabulary, not whether the value works. It crashed on every run for six days (a `parts` shape
# change it never learned) and nobody noticed, because it lived in neither this file nor CI. It is here
# now, and its own adoption ratchet (quality/baselines/coverage-ratchet.json) fails the push if engine
# adoption FALLS, the direction that matters: a capability the library used to reach going back to zero
# is a regression the same way a broken test is. It is NOT in gates.yml for the reason doc-refs is not:
# films/scene/*.json is gitignored, so a CI clone sees a fraction of the AUTHORED library and would
# ratchet against a population it cannot see. Pure file reads, well under a second.
# docker-check is here for the reason every other gate on this line is: the failure it catches is
# invisible from the repo and from every local run. A COPY of a path git does not carry builds fine on
# a machine that has the path and dies on the server, which has only the clone. assets/plinth was
# untracked on 2026-09-10 and the COPY stayed, so every deploy after that failed while the running
# container kept serving a stale site for ten days. The gate was already written and correct; nothing
# ran it. It reads two files and costs milliseconds.
# push-guard runs FIRST and reads the refs git hands this hook on stdin, so it checks exactly what is
# about to reach the remote. It is first because the two rules it holds are about publication rather
# than quality: a wrong gate result costs a re-run, an attribution trailer or a force-added binary in a
# public repo costs a history rewrite. Both have already happened once.
#
# It scans the whole pushed RANGE rather than the working tree, because the incident arrived by MERGE:
# the commit was authored in an agent's worktree, where this machine's commit-msg hook may not have run
# at all, and reached main through the lead.
#
# deploy-check runs in the same loop, on the same range, for the same reason push-guard does: it needs
# to know what is actually about to reach the remote, not what the working tree happens to hold. It is
# quality/gates/site-build-check.mjs: it runs the site's real build (site-engine, the fonts generator,
# tsc --noEmit) and costs about 3 seconds, but ONLY when the pushed range actually touches something the
# site build reads (derived from the Dockerfile's own COPY lines). A push that never touches the site
# pays nothing. This is the local half of the ten-day vawe.dev outage: three stacked build breaks that
# every local check passed, because none of them ran the build.
#
# The eight below joined together (2026-09-22, quality/gates/inert-check.mjs found all of them standing
# in the Makefile and reached by neither this file nor CI). Each needs a file a fresh CI clone does not
# carry (a source woff2, a baked sim, a judge receipt) or is the check that found the other seventeen, so
# they belong here rather than in gates.yml: glyphs-audit (assets/fonts/*.woff2, gitignored), sim-audit
# and docker-context (assets/baked, gitignored, both self-heal to a pass when it is absent rather than
# guessing), judge-census (quality/baselines/approved/judge/, gitignored, a count only). rung, sfx-check,
# audit-test and inert-check carry no such dependency; they are here because pre-push is where a push-time
# check that already passes belongs, same as the fourteen above them. All eight pass today. Pure file
# reads plus, for sim-audit and glyphs-audit, one hash each; well under a second combined.
#
# skill-check and rule-length joined the same day inert-check found them: both in the Makefile, both
# read only tracked docs (skills/*/SKILL.md, AGENTS.md, engine-doctrine/CRAFT), both pass today, both
# under a tenth of a second. Neither is in gates.yml, matching the rest of this maintenance tier
# (skill-reach, rung, provenance): pre-push and CI do not mirror past the fast job gates.yml itself
# names, and these belong with their siblings.
while read -r _local_ref local_sha _remote_ref remote_sha; do
  [ "$local_sha" = "0000000000000000000000000000000000000000" ] && continue   # a branch deletion
  if [ "$remote_sha" = "0000000000000000000000000000000000000000" ]; then
    range="$local_sha"                     # a new branch: every commit it carries is new to the remote
  else
    range="$remote_sha..$local_sha"
  fi
  if ! node harness/dev/push-guard.mjs "$range"; then
    echo "✗ pre-push blocked by push-guard. These are house rules, not style checks." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if ! node quality/gates/site-build-check.mjs "$range"; then
    echo "✗ pre-push blocked: the site would not have built. Fix it, or --no-verify to override." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
done

echo "▶ pre-push: framework gates (schema-check + lib-test + craft-coverage + coverage + doc-refs + skill-reach + arsenal + discovery + generated + scenes + mistakes + docker + prop-probe + seo-surface + rung + provenance + sfx-check + audit-test + glyphs-audit + sim-audit + docker-context + judge-census + inert-check + skill-check + rule-length) …"
if ! make schema-check lib-test craft-coverage coverage doc-refs skill-reach arsenal-check discovery generated-check scenes-json no-emdash mistakes-check docker-check prop-probe seo-surface rung provenance sfx-check audit-test glyphs-audit sim-audit docker-context judge-census inert-check skill-check rule-length; then
  echo "✗ pre-push blocked: framework check failed. Fix it, or push with --no-verify to override." >&2
  exit 1
fi
echo "✓ pre-push: framework healthy"

# ci-status is informational only, never a gate: it reports the LAST CI run (this push has no run yet)
# so a red gates.yml is visible right here instead of discovered by nobody looking. One API call, never
# polled. See harness/dev/ci-status.mjs for why this exists (CI was red for four runs, unnoticed).
node harness/dev/ci-status.mjs || true
